hi everyone our colleagues in New York  asked us to do a reporter's notebook so  on behalf of my team we were kind of  sitting here thinking where exactly do  you begin right we've been on the island  for roughly 48 hours so I thought we'd  start where it all ended for us it ends  here in uma kau we just filed our last  report for the CBS Evening News and uma  cows on the southeastern part of the  island and as I look out I'm reminded  that I want to come back here at some  point for a vacation  that's Las Palmas the orange roofs and  sort of the buildings that are farthest  in the distance from where we are the  closest to the water but I also want to  point out how green and lush things are  here might not seem like a big deal but  I remember after the hurricane  everything was brown and I mean  everything  in fact it reminded me of covering  wildfires in Southern California our  time here has been productive exhausting  at moments haven't slept much and have  an incredible team of people you may  have heard me say that there's no  television without the people behind the  scenes right so what places we went to  while we were here ooh tato salinas de  boku wa uma cow san juan cog was in fact  in congress I want to tell you it was  actually our security guard who  mentioned that there were people in the  area who were helping to direct traffic  driving around the island one thing that  I noticed six months later is that most  of the traffic lights are not working  but the people who are directing traffic  are locals they're not police officers  they're not government workers  I'm told that homeless people have  stepped in to help and regular folks who  either don't have a job or in between  jobs have put on a reflective vest grab  the whistle and are directing traffic at  intersections we spoke to a man who said  he gets paid tipped regularly by drivers  they rolled down the window we even saw  a police officer who stopped at a light  and listened to the directions of this  gentleman who was directing traffic I  mean it's sort of just an accepted way  of life here on the island and it and  sort of a sign again of what we've seen  over the last six months which is Puerto  Ricans depending more on each other than  they depend on their government a lot of  people will tell you they have been  disappointed by  actions of the government the government  will tell you it was a storm of epic  proportions right it was a it was a  storm that they anticipated but the  infrastructure on the island just could  not withstand it  speaking of infrastructure it's worth  it's it's coming to mind I don't know if  many of you are aware but the Army Corps  of Engineers that's working here on the  island working alongside prepa is  operating under the Stafford Act and the  Stafford Act actually prevents them from  doing any redesigns or upgrades to the  grid so many of you have reached out to  me on social media and said David is the  grid going to be better after the work  is done is it going to be better than  what it was before Berea and the answer  is no it's gonna be the exact same and  here's why the Stafford Act authorizes  the US Army Corps of Engineers to bring  in brigades and and workers to make  repairs where they need to be and  replacements where necessary right so if  a pole is not demolished and can be  repaired they just repair it if the pole  is you know decimated then they put a  new pole in but they're not gonna  redesign that grid in that municipality  to be better stronger or more resilient  because the Stafford Act doesn't  authorize that some other highlights for  our trip this trip was actually the  first time I got to tato the bridge has  been rebuilt and that bridge is  essential I mean you had thousands of  people in the mountains who were cut off  from civilization one lady told me she  was actually unable to get out of her  house initially after the storm in  hooter Watteau for about three weeks  couldn't even leave her home not to  mention actually get down the mountain  to try and cross the bridge I also  wanted to make a point in coming here of  not just highlighting the bad stuff  there's a whole lot of good things that  are happening happening in fact when we  rode around the island what I was struck  by is how everything seemed to be open  and people seemed to be going about  their daily life with little with little  interruption  so most people were working in fact talk  about a big change for us as we saw  interviews sought out interviews with  folks we were told by many people oh  they're at work oh they're working  they're on time for enter  yes right when we were here after the  storm everybody a time because  everybody's job was shutdown nothing was  working but we actually had some trouble  trying to get folks to talk because they  were working that's a good sign no more  lines to get money at the bank no more  lines to get gas we went to the smallest  areas actually coming from  we went Salinas Caguas down to Ponce a  and sort of between no where do we go  how do we what was that gas station I  think was outside upon say in a very  rural area at any rate in a very rural  area stopped by a gas station and they  had fueled no long lines and those small  really rural stations were the ones who  were sort of some of the last to get it  so to speak we didn't spend much time in  San Juan on this trip in fact what I did  when we when we started this trip was we  were sitting down talking about what  stories and wanted to do and I said you  know we have such a loyal audience that  has followed our reporting since the  beginning let's reach out to them and  see what we should do and there was  actually a college professor who took a  look at all of the responses and made a  list and said of the responses back to  David beg no people said go to these and  at the top of the list was uma cow the  java KOA and to otto and we got to all  of those places so i also heard from  many of you don't go to san juan things  are fine and so we didn't do much in san  juan because we wanted to make sure we  got out into the rural areas where there  are more issues right some other  highlights the governor announced  actually on the anniversary the  six-month anniversary of hurricane maria  that he was establishing a rainy day  fund and I called his spokeswoman to get  more information and essentially what  she told me was this rainy day fund is  going to be used to help bring in  brigades electrical brigades faster  right because you might remember after  the storm it took so long to get repair  crews here and it not only took time to  get crews here but it took even more  time to get the supplies that the crews  needed here right so they got the men on  the island to do the power work but they  needed the poles and it took weeks in  some cases months I mean it was  agonizing period speaking about that our  member interviewing Colonel Kirk of the  US Army Corps of Engineers who spoke  quite candidly and said you know David  my dad asked me why don't you guys put  the power poles underground that's how  we do it stateside why don't you make it  safer and more resilient in Puerto Rico  and he said I told my dad number one we  can't because we're operating out of the  Stafford Act but he said number two if  we tried to do that all around the  island we could have taken us a year or  more to restore power because it would  have taken so much time so anyway that's  kind of everything that's on the top of  my head in my heart right now I'll end  with something kind of comical at least  it is to me  so lin-manuel Miranda sent out a tweet  earlier today saying I was an honorary  Puerto Rican and by the way somebody  asked me the other day aren't you from  Puerto Rico and I said not only am I not  from Puerto Rico I had never been a  Puerto Rico and I didn't know what Porto  Rican before the hurricane but any rate  Lind sent out a tweet today saying I was  an honorary Puerto Rican and thanking  CBS for our work and he said you know  David you won't have to pay for and I  hope I pronounce this correctly past  Dallas but did I say it right past Ellis  you'll appreciate this our fixer who is  a local from the island when I said it  the wrong way he kind of grimaced but at  any rate I had to respond I said I don't  even know what that is but what I do  know is that a slice of my heart will  always remain in Puerto Rico we are  grateful to the people of Puerto Rico  the Americans who live here for allowing  us to tell their story and we will  continue to tell those stories online on  broadcast and on social media I am I am  hell-bent on not letting people forget  about what's happening here in Puerto  Rico so thanks for listening I hope it  was worth your time and I'll see you on  social media and on TV of course
 

